target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestAnEmptyAskedDictIsFalsy'
expect: an empty dict read as truthy
why:    an empty collection is falsy in Python whether it is a list or a dict, and the two need separate entries: one entry deleting BOTH cases together could not tell them apart, so removing only the map case compiled and passed every test and parity fixture. Without it a dict-valued `asked` or `analysis_changed` falls to Go's `v != nil` default, which is true for a non-nil empty map, and blocks a convergence the Python grants — the inverse of the list bug this pair exists for.
--- old
	case map[string]any:
		return len(t) != 0 // Python: an empty dict is falsy
--- new
	case map[string]any:
		return v != nil // the default branch's behaviour, restored for dicts only
